[SIZE=5][B][COLOR=#2980B9]Core Architectural Pillars[/COLOR][/B][/SIZE]
RTP is engineered from the ground up to solve the fundamental performance and reliability problems that plague other teleportation plugins. Our design is built on four key pillars:

[LIST]
[*][B]Deterministic Selection Engine:[/B] We use a specialized [B]spiral mapping algorithm[/B] to explore your world. This eliminates lag-inducing "re-roll" guessing and guarantees fast, uniform player distribution even in massive worlds.
[*][B]Platform-Native Performance:[/B] RTP isn't just "compatible with" modern servers—it's [B]optimized for them[/B]. With dedicated support for Spigot, Paper, and Folia, we use the best background processing and threading models each platform has to offer.
[*][B]Active Resource Watchdog:[/B] A persistent background watchdog actively monitors all tasks and chunk requests, instantly reclaiming resources from abandoned teleports and preventing memory leaks before they can start.
[*][B]Direct Region I/O Pre-Filter:[/B] On Spigot [I]and Folia[/I], we bypass slow, region-bound chunk loads by reading world data directly from disk (`.mca` region files) on a background thread pool. This gives you Paper-grade off-tick safety evaluation on Spigot and eliminates per-candidate Region-Thread hops on Folia.
[/LIST]

[CENTER][COLOR=#D1D1D1]━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━[/COLOR][/CENTER]

[SIZE=5][B][COLOR=#16A085]Chunk Loading — How It Actually Works On Your Platform[/COLOR][/B][/SIZE]
Chunk I/O is the single biggest source of teleport lag. Most RTP plugins treat every platform the same and pay the cost of the [I]worst[/I] one. RTP does not. Here is exactly what happens when a candidate chunk is evaluated on your server:

[LIST]
[*][B][COLOR=#27AE60]Paper — Fully Async, Zero Tradeoffs.[/COLOR][/B] Paper exposes a native `World#getChunkAtAsync` that returns a `CompletableFuture<Chunk>`. RTP uses it directly. Safety evaluation runs off-tick. No pre-filter needed, no main-thread fallback, no surprises. [B]This is the reference platform.[/B]
[*][B][COLOR=#2980B9]Folia — Off-Tick Pre-Filter + Native Region Scheduler.[/COLOR][/B] Folia's chunk API is Region-Thread-bound, which normally means every candidate costs a scheduler hop. RTP defeats this by running the [B]Anvil read-only pre-filter[/B] on `ForkJoinPool.commonPool()` [I]before[/I] touching the Region Scheduler — rejected candidates never hop a thread. Confirmed candidates are then loaded via Folia's native async region-aware API. Teleports themselves are dispatched through the Entity Scheduler per Folia's contract.
[*][B][COLOR=#E67E22]Spigot — Anvil Pre-Filter Does The Heavy Lifting.[/COLOR][/B] Vanilla Spigot has [I]no[/I] `CompletableFuture`-returning async chunk API — the `World#getChunkAtAsync(int,int) -> CompletableFuture<Chunk>` overload is a Paper addition. Without mitigation, every candidate would drive a synchronous main-thread `getChunkAt`. RTP mitigates this by parsing `.mca` region files directly off-tick: `isAir`, `isSafe`, surface-height and sky-light checks all run on `ForkJoinPool.commonPool()` against an `AnvilChunkView`. [B]This is how Spigot gets Paper-class selection throughput without a Paper fork.[/B]
[*][B][COLOR=#C0392B]Honest Caveats (Spigot + Folia fallback path).[/COLOR][/B] The Anvil pre-filter is a [I]data source[/I], not a universal gate. It falls through to the platform's native chunk API when: (1) the chunk is already loaded (live data wins), (2) the world uses a custom `ChunkGenerator` (Iris, Terra, etc.), or (3) the Anvil format is unrecognized. On Spigot, the fallback is a main-thread `getChunkAt` scheduled via the primary scheduler — unblocking the caller's future but not moving the I/O off-tick. On Folia, the fallback is a Region-Scheduler hop. [B]If you run a custom-generator world on Spigot, you will not get the off-tick win for those chunks — use Paper or Folia for that workload.[/B]
[*][B][COLOR=#8E44AD]Fabric — Not Supported.[/COLOR][/B] Fabric is on the roadmap but is explicitly out of scope for the current release. Do not run RTP on a Fabric server and expect production behavior.
[/LIST]

[B]Operator takeaway:[/B] on Paper and Folia, chunk I/O is fully off-tick by design. On vanilla Spigot, the Anvil pre-filter covers the common case (unloaded chunks, vanilla generator) — which is the vast majority of candidates in a typical selection sweep — and the rare fallback to main-thread `getChunkAt` is bounded by your configured tick-budget. No platform silently blocks the tick loop without reporting it.

